+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af895eee52b2a0b5","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Factory Reset\n     2→\n     3→Solve button-toggle puzzles for a factory machine control panel.\n     4→\n     5→## Part 1: Toggle Puzzle\n     6→\n     7→A machine has N lights, all initially **off** (false). There are M buttons. Each button, when pressed, **toggles** a specific set of lights (off→on, on→off). Each button can be pressed **at most once** (0 or 1 presses).\n     8→\n     9→Find the minimum set of buttons to press so that the lights match a target configuration.\n    10→\n    11→### Data Model\n    12→\n    13→```typescript\n    14→interface Machine {\n    15→  target: boolean[];     // desired state for each light (all start off)\n    16→  buttons: number[][];   // buttons[i] = array of light indices toggled by button i\n    17→}\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→### API\n    21→\n    22→```typescript\n    23→function solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null;\n    24→```\n    25→\n    26→- Return an array of **button indices** (0-based) to press, sorted in ascending order.\n    27→- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **fewest button presses**.\n    28→- If tied on press count, return the **lexicographically smallest** set of indices.\n    29→- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n    30→\n    31→### Example\n    32→\n    33→```\n    34→Lights: 4 (indices 0-3), all start off\n    35→Target: [true, true, false, true]\n    36→Buttons:\n    37→  0: toggles lights [0, 1]\n    38→  1: toggles lights [1, 2, 3]\n    39→  2: toggles lights [0, 3]\n    40→\n    41→Press buttons 0 and 1:\n    42→  Button 0: lights become [on, on, off, off]\n    43→  Button 1: lights become [on, off, on, on]\n    44→  Wait — that gives [true, false, true, true], not the target.\n    45→\n    46→Press buttons 1 and 2:\n    47→  Button 1: [off, on, on, on]\n    48→  Button 2: [on, on, on, off]\n    49→  That gives [true, true, true, false], also wrong.\n    50→\n    51→Press buttons 0, 1, 2:\n    52→  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n    53→  After 1: [on, off, on, on]\n    54→  After 2: [off, off, on, off]\n    55→  Wrong again.\n    56→\n    57→Correct: press buttons 0 and 2:\n    58→  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n    59→  After 2: [off, on, off, on]\n    60→  Result: [false, true, false, true]\n    61→  Hmm, that's not the target either.\n    62→\n    63→Actually, let's re-check: target is [true, true, false, true].\n    64→  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [on, off, off, on]\n    65→  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, on, on, off]\n    66→  Not matching.\n    67→\n    68→  Press button 0: toggles [0, 1] → [on, on, off, off]\n    69→  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, off, on, on]\n    70→  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [off, off, on, off]\n    71→  Still wrong.\n    72→\n    73→The toggle order doesn't matter (toggling is commutative). So for target [T, T, F, T]:\n    74→We need light 0=on, 1=on, 2=off, 3=on.\n    75→Button 0 affects {0,1}, Button 1 affects {1,2,3}, Button 2 affects {0,3}.\n    76→We need: b0 XOR b2 = 1 (light 0), b0 XOR b1 = 1 (light 1), b1 = 0 (light 2), b1 XOR b2 = 1 (light 3).\n    77→From light 2: b1=0. From light 1: b0=1. From light 0: b2=0. Check light 3: 0 XOR 0 = 0 ≠ 1. No solution!\n    78→\n    79→solveToggle returns null.\n    80→```\n    81→\n    82→**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons and 40 lights and return results in under 5 seconds. Brute-force enumeration of all 2^40 button combinations is not feasible.\n    83→\n    84→## Part 2: Counter Puzzle\n    85→\n    86→A variant where lights are replaced by **counters**. Each counter starts at 0. Buttons **increment** (not toggle) specific counters by 1. Counters wrap around at a given modulus (e.g., mod 5 means values cycle 0→1→2→3→4→0).\n    87→\n    88→Each button can be pressed **multiple times** (0 to modulus-1 times).\n    89→\n    90→```typescript\n    91→function solveCounter(\n    92→  lights: number[],      // target counter values (each 0..modulus-1)\n    93→  buttons: number[][],   // buttons[i] = indices of counters affected\n    94→  modulus: number        // counters wrap at this value (always prime)\n    95→): number[] | null;\n    96→```\n    97→\n    98→- Return an array where `result[i]` is the number of times button `i` should be pressed (0 to modulus-1).\n    99→- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **minimum total presses** (sum of all result values).\n   100→- If tied on total, return the **lexicographically smallest** result array.\n   101→- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n   102→- The modulus is always a **prime number**.\n   103→\n   104→**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons, 40 counters, and modulus up to 97. Must complete in under 5 seconds.\n   105→\n   106→## Commands\n   107→\n   108→```bash\n   109→npm test        # run tests\n   110→npm run build   # compile TypeScript\n   111→npm run lint    # lint source\n   112→```\n   113→\n   114→## File Structure\n   115→\n   116→Place your implementation in `src/`. The main entry point should be `src/index.ts`.\n   117→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3ca29dac453279d","session_id":"90e40719-fcc8-4f73-86e6-c94be474e43e","uuid":"a0868ed9-884b-4840-9337-a7d8253e6923"}
