Gaussian elimination over GF(2):\n\n1. We perform row reduction on the augmented matrix [A | b].\n2. If any row has all zeros in A but b = 1, the system is inconsistent → return null.\n3. Otherwise, we get a particular solution and identify free variables.\n4. The general solution is: x_pivot[i] = c_i + sum_j(a_{ij} * f_j) for each pivot i, and x_free[j] = f_j for each free variable j.\n5. We want to minimize |x| and then choose the lexicographically smallest solution.\n\nFor step 5, with k free variables, we need to evaluate 2^k possible assignments. For k ≤ 25, brute force works. For larger k, we need optimization.\n\nBut let me consider: in practice, with 40 lights and 40 buttons, if the matrix has rank close to 40, k is small. If the matrix has low rank, we have many free variables but also many constraints are trivially satisfied, which limits the search space.\n\nLet me just implement the brute force approach for now and optimize later if needed.\n\nActually, let me think about the meet-in-the-middle approach one more time.\n\nFor the toggle problem with k free variables:\n- Split into two halves of size k1 and k2\n- For the first half, enumerate all 2^k1 assignments and compute:\n  - The partial solution vector (only the pivot components affected by the first half)\n  - The number of 1s among the free variables in the first half\n  - The partial contribution to each pivot variable\n- For the second half, similarly enumerate all 2^k2 assignments\n- To combine, for each pair, the total weight is: (number of 1s in pivot variables) + (number of 1s in free variables from first half) + (number of 1s in free variables from second half)\n\nThe pivot variable i is 1 if (c_i XOR contribution_from_A XOR contribution_from_B) = 1.\n\nIf I represent the contributions as bitmasks (over the pivot variables), then for each assignment of the first half, I get a bitmask B_A and a weight w_A. For each assignment of the second half, I get a bitmask B_B and a weight w_B. The total