 the counter problem. For the counter problem:\n1. Gaussian elimination over GF(p) where p is prime\n2. After elimination, we have the solution space\n3. Free variables can take values 0 to p-1\n4. With k free variables and modulus p, there are p^k possible solutions\n5. We want to minimize the sum of all x_i values, then the lexicographically smallest x array\n\nFor the counter problem, with p up to 97 and k up to 40, p^k can be enormous. But with Gaussian elimination, k should be manageable.\n\nActually, for the counter problem, the approach is different because we're minimizing the sum of x values, not the number of non-zero values. After Gaussian elimination:\n- Pivot variables are determined by free variables: p_i = c_i + sum_j(a_{ij} * f_j) (mod p)\n- We want to minimize sum_i(p_i) + sum_j(f_j) where all values are taken mod p (but the sum is over the actual values 0 to p-1)\n\nWait, the sum should be over the actual values (0 to p-1), not modular values. So sum_i(x_i) where x_i ∈ {0, 1, ..., p-1}.\n\nThis is an optimization problem over the free variables. For each free variable f_j ∈ {0, ..., p-1}, the pivot variables are determined. The total sum is:\ntotal = sum_i(p_i(f_1, ..., f_k)) + sum_j(f_j)\n\nThis is a function of k variables, each with p possible values. The total search space is p^k. For p = 97 and k = 40, this is way too much.\n\nBut again, with 40 lights and 40 buttons, the rank should be high enough that k is small. Let me implement a basic solution and optimize later.\n\nActually, for the counter problem with the lexicographic tiebreaker on the full result array (not just the sorted list), I need to be more careful. The result is an array of length M (number of buttons), where result[i] is the number of times button i is pressed. Among solutions with the minimum total presses, we want the lexicographically smallest result array.\n\nThis means: prefer result[0] to be as small as possible, then result[1], etc. So we prefer pressing earlier buttons fewer times.\n\nThis is different from the toggle problem where we prefer pressing earlier buttons (setting them to 1).\n\nOK let me just start coding. I'll implement:\n1. Gaussian elimination over GF(2) for the toggle problem\n2. Gaussian elimination over GF(p) for the counter problem\n3. Optimization of free variables for both\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nFor the toggle problem, here's my implementation plan:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Machine {\n  target: boolean[];\n  buttons: number[][];\n}\n\nfunction solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null {\n  const n = machine.target.length; // number of lights\n  const m = machine.buttons.length; // number of buttons\n  \n  // Build augmented matrix [A | b] over GF(2)\n  // A[i][j] = 1 if button j toggles light i\n  // b[i] = 1 if target[i] is true\n  \n  const matrix: number[][] = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const row = new Array(m + 1).fill(0);\n    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n      if (machine.buttons[j].includes(i)) {\n        row[j] = 1;\n      }\n    }\n    row[m] = machine.target[i] ? 1 : 0;\n    matrix.push(row);\n  }\n  \n  // Gaussian elimination\n  const pivotCol: number[] = []; // pivotCol[row] = column of pivot in this row\n  const pivotRow: number[] = new Array(m).fill(-1); // pivotRow[col] = row of pivot in this column\n  let row = 0;\n  \n  for (let col = 0; col < m && row < n; col++) {\n    // Find pivot\n    let pivot = -1;\n    for (let i = row; i < n; i++) {\n      if (matrix[i][col] === 1) {\n        pivot = i;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    if (pivot === -1) continue; // free variable\n    \n    // Swap rows\n    [matrix[row], matrix[pivot]] = [matrix[pivot], matrix[row]];\n    \n    // Eliminate\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      if (i !== row && matrix[i][col] === 1) {\n        for (let j = col; j <= m; j++) {\n          matrix[i][j] ^= matrix[row][j];\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    pivotCol.push(col);\n    pivotRow[col] = row;\n    row++;\n  }\n  \n  const rank = row;\n  \n  // Check consistency: any row with all zeros in A but b = 1?\n  for (let i = rank; i < n; i++) {\n    if (matrix[i][m] === 1) return null;\n  }\n  \n  // Identify free variables\n  const freeVars: number[] = [];\n  for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n    if (pivotRow[j] === -1) freeVars.push(j);\n  }\n  \n  const k = freeVars.length;\n  \n  // For each free variable assignment, compute the solution\n  // Pivot variable values: x[pivotCol[r]] = matrix[r][m] XOR sum_{j free} matrix[r][j] * f_j\n  \n  let bestWeight = Infinity;\n  let bestSolution: number[] | null = null;\n  \n  // Enumerate all 2^k free variable assignments\n  for (let mask = 0; mask < (1 << k); mask++) {\n    const x = new Array(m).fill(0);\n    \n    // Set free variables\n    for (let j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n      x[freeVars[j]] = (mask >> j) & 1;\n    }\n    \n    // Compute pivot variables\n    for (let r = 0; r < rank; r++) {\n      const col = pivotCol[r];\n      x[col] = matrix[r][m];\n      for (let j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n        if (matrix[r][freeVars[j]] === 1) {\n          x[col] ^= (mask >> j) & 1;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Compute weight\n    const pressed = x.reduce((acc, v, i) => v ? [...acc, i] : acc, [] as number[]);\n    const weight = pressed.length;\n    \n    if (weight < bestWeight || (weight === bestWeight && lexCompare(pressed, bestSolution!) < 0)) {\n      bestWeight = weight;\n      bestSolution = pressed;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return bestSolution;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this won't handle the case where k > 25 efficiently. Let me think about optimization.\n\nFor k > 25, I can use meet-in-the-middle:\n- Split free variables into two halves A (size k1) and B (size k2)\n- For each of 2^k1 assignments of A, compute the partial contribution to each pivot variable and the free variable values\n- For each of 2^k2 assignments of B, similarly\n- Combine to find the minimum weight solution\n\nThe key insight is: the pivot variables depend on both halves. But we can compute the contribution from each half separately:\n- Let p_r = matrix[r][m] XOR (contribution from A) XOR (contribution from B)\n- Where contribution from A depends only on f_A and contribution from B depends only on f_B\n\nFor the toggle problem, p_r = c_r XOR a_r XOR b_r, where:\n- c_r = matrix[r][m]\n- a_r = XOR of (matrix[r][freeVars[j]] * f_j) for j in A\n- b_r = XOR of (matrix[r][freeVars[j]] * f_j) for j in B\n\nSo p_r = c_r XOR a_r XOR b_r.\n\nThe weight is: sum_r (c_r XOR a_r XOR b_r) + sum_{j in A} f_j + sum_{j in B} f_j.\n\nLet's define: base_r = c_r XOR a_r. Then p_r = base_r XOR b_r.\n\nFor a fixed assignment of A:\n- base_r is determined for all r\n- weight = sum_r (base_r XOR b_r) + w_A + w_B\n\nwhere w_A = sum_{j in A} f_j and w_B = sum_{j in B} f_j.\n\nFor a fixed base and w_A, we want to minimize:\n  sum_r (base_r XOR b_r) + w_B\n\nover all assignments of B.\n\nThis is equivalent to: for each assignment of B, compute the total contribution, and find the minimum.\n\nBut we still need to consider all 2^k2 assignments of B for each of the 2^k1 assignments of A. Total: 2^k1 * 2^k2 = 2^k. No improvement.\n\nHmm. Let me think differently. What if we use a different splitting?\n\nActually, for the minimum weight codeword problem, the standard meet-in-the-middle approach is:\n\n1. Split the n positions into two halves: first n1 positions and last n2 positions.\n2. Enumerate all codewords and split them into the first n1 and last n2 bits.\n3. For each codeword, the first half must match a particular pattern, and the second half must match another.\n\nBut this doesn't directly apply to our problem.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Instead of meet-in-the-middle on the free variables, let me use meet-in-the-middle on the buttons themselves.\n\nActually, I think the simplest practical approach is:\n1. Use brute force for k ≤ 25\n2. For k > 25, use a different strategy\n\nFor k > 25, we can observe that the problem is equivalent to: given the null s