eturn null).\n\nAlso, for the toggle problem, if all lights are off (target is all false), the answer is pressing no buttons (return []).\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- No buttons: if target is all false/off, return []; otherwise null\n- No lights: if target is empty, return []\n- More buttons than lights: might have many free variables\n- More lights than buttons: might have no solution\n\nOK, let me start coding.\n\nLet me write the code for both functions.\n\n```typescript\nexport interface Machine {\n  target: boolean[];\n  buttons: number[][];\n}\n\nexport function solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null {\n  const n = machine.target.length; // number of lights\n  const m = machine.buttons.length; // number of buttons\n  \n  if (n === 0) return [];\n  if (m === 0) {\n    return machine.target.every(t => !t) ? [] : null;\n  }\n  \n  // Meet-in-the-middle on buttons\n  const m1 = Math.floor(m / 2);\n  const m2 = m - m1;\n  \n  // For each subset of buttons 0..m1-1, compute the resulting light pattern\n  // Store: lightPattern -> { size, indices } (best subset for this pattern)\n  const map = new Map<bigint, { size: number; indices: number[] }>();\n  \n  for (let mask = 0; mask < (1 << m1); mask++) {\n    const pattern = new Uint8Array(n);\n    const indices: number[] = [];\n    let size = 0;\n    \n    for (let j = 0; j < m1; j++) {\n      if (mask & (1 << j)) {\n        indices.push(j);\n        size++;\n        for (const light of machine.buttons[j]) {\n          pattern[light] ^= 1;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Convert pattern to bigint key\n    let key = 0n;\n    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n      key = key * 2n + BigInt(pattern[i]);\n    }\n    \n    const existing = map.get(key);\n    if (!existing || size < existing.size || (size === existing.size && lexCompare(indices, existing.indices) < 0)) {\n      map.set(key, { size, indices });\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Target pattern as bigint\n  let targetKey = 0n;\n  for (let i = 