rrect.\n\nBut the problem probably won't have duplicate indices. I'll just use the bitmask approach.\n\nLet me also think about whether buttons can be empty (buttons[i] = []). In that case, pressing the button has no effect. The meet-in-the-middle handles this correctly.\n\nOK, I'm going to write the code now. No more deliberation.\n\nLet me write the complete src/index.ts:\n\n```typescript\nexport interface Machine {\n  target: boolean[];\n  buttons: number[][];\n}\n\n/**\n * Compare two bitmasks by the lexicographic order of their set bit positions.\n * Returns negative if a < b, 0 if equal, positive if a > b.\n */\nfunction compareLex(a: number, b: number): number {\n  while (a !== 0 || b !== 0) {\n    if (a === 0) return 1;  // a has fewer bits, so a's list is shorter → a < b? No!\n    if (b === 0) return -1;\n    const lowA = a & (-a);\n    const lowB = b & (-b);\n    if (lowA !== lowB) return lowA - lowB;\n    a ^= lowA;\n    b ^= lowB;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the compareLex function. If a = 0 (no bits set, empty list) and b ≠ 0 (some bits set, non-empty list), which is lexicographically smaller?\n\nAn empty list [] is lexicographically smaller than any non-empty list [x, ...] because the empty list is a prefix. So if a = 0 and b ≠ 0, then a < b → return negative.\n\nBut in my code, if a = 0 and b ≠ 0, I return 1 (positive), which means a > b. That's wrong!\n\nLet me fix this:\n\n```\nfunction compareLex(a: number, b: number): number {\n  while (a !== 0 || b !== 0) {\n    if (a === 0) return -1;  // a is empty, b is not → a < b\n    if (b === 0) return 1;   // b is empty, a is not → b < a\n    const lowA = a & (-a);\n    const lowB = b & (-b);\n    if (lowA !== lowB) return lowA - lowB;\n    a ^= lowA;\n    b ^= lowB;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nWait, is an empty set lexicographically smaller than a non-empty set? The problem says the result is a sorted array of button indices. An empty array [] is lexicographically smaller than [0] or any non-empty array. Yes, that's correct.\n\nBut wait, in the context of Part 1, the press count is the primary criterion. If the press count is 0, the result is []. If the press count is > 0, the result is a non-empty array. We never compare an empty array with a non-empty array in the lexicographic tiebreaker because they have different press counts.\n\nHmm, actually, we do compare within the same light state in the first-half map. Two first-half entries for the same light state could have different press counts. We keep the one with fewer presses, so the empty set (0 presses) would be preferred over any non-empty set (1+ presses). The lexicographic tiebreaker is only used when press counts are equal. So we never compare an empty set with a non-empty set in the tiebreaker.\n\nBut it's still good to get the comparison function right. Let me think about it again.\n\nActually wait, in the context of comparing first-half entries for the same light state in the map, I keep the best entry. Two entries with different press counts: the one with fewer presses wins. Two entries with the same press count: the one with the lexicographically smaller index set wins.\n\nFor two entries with the same press count > 0, I compare their index sets lexicographically. Both are non-empty.\n\nFor two entries with the same press count = 0, both are empty. They're equal.\n\nSo the compareLex function for non-empty sets is:\n\n```\nfunction compareLex(a: number, b: number): number {\n  // Both a and b are non-zero (non-empty sets)\n  while (a !== 0 && b !== 0) {\n    const lowA = a & (-a);\n    const lowB = b & (-b);\n    if (lowA !== lowB) return lowA - lowB;\n    a ^= lowA;\n    b ^= lowB;\n  }\n  if (a === 0 && b === 0) return 0;\n  if (a === 0) return -1; // a is a prefix of b → a < b\n  return 1; // b is a prefix of a → b < a\n}\n```\n\nThis handles the case where one set is a subset of the other. For example, {0} vs {0, 1}: a = 1, b = 3. After removing the common bit (1), a =