nt;\n    bestFirstMask = fentry.mask;\n    bestSecondMask = smask;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the comparison logic is getting complex. Let me simplify by writing a combined comparison function.\n\n```\nfunction compareSolution(\n  f1: number, s1: number, c1: number,\n  f2: number, s2: number, c2: number\n): number {\n  if (c1 !== c2) return c1 - c2;\n  const fc = compareLex(f1, f2);\n  if (fc !== 0) return fc;\n  return compareLex(s1, s2);\n}\n```\n\nWhere f1, s1 are first and second half masks, c1 is total press count.\n\nOK, I think I have a good plan now. Let me write the code.\n\nActually, one more optimization for Part 1. Instead of using a Map<number, {count, mask}>, I can use a plain object or a Map. In JavaScript, Map with number keys is efficient.\n\nBut for 2^20 entries, the Map might be slow due to hashing overhead. An alternative is to use an array indexed by the light state. But the light state is a 40-bit number, so an array would be too large (2^40 entries).\n\nI'll stick with the Map approach. 2^20 entries with number keys should be fast enough.\n\nActually, let me think about whether I should use a regular object instead of a Map. In V8, Map with number keys is optimized and should be efficient. I'll use Map.\n\nOK, let me write the complete code now. I'll be concise and avoid over-engineering.\n\nLet me also think about what to do for the case where both halves have 0 buttons (m = 0). In this case, 2^0 = 1, and the map has {0: {0, 0}}. The second-half enumeration also has 1 combination. We check if targetMask ^ 0 = targetMask is in the map, which is only true if targetMask = 0. Correct.\n\nAnd for m = 1: mid = 0, m1 = 0, m2 = 1. First half has 0 buttons, map has {0: {0, 0}}. Second half has 1 button, 2 combinations. We check if targetMask ^ 0 or targetMask ^ toggleMasks[0] is 0. Correct.\n\nWait, mid = Math.floor(1/2) = 0. So m1 = 0, m2 = 1. First half: buttons[0..0-1] = empty. Second half: buttons[0..0] = button 0.\n\nFor the second half, mask = 0 gives